What have we done design wise?
-------------------------------
The adversary never sends tokens to fMulticast when delivering a message to someone, i.e. never pays for it

similarly, right now in bangFtoken the adverasry isn't charged anything --> this may be becaus adversaries right now don't track their own tokens.

When the protocol parties or fMulticast don't have enough tokens to continue
they do a best effort and send as many tokens as they can. This is not the
intended behavior of ITMs as ideally they should halt. However, to facilitate
termination of the program, in order for tests to detect a failure, we need
that contorl always returns back to the environment rather than machine
shalting and the write token being stuck in some other ITM indefinitely.

Changed require in protACast so that when a require failsthe machine doesn't
just halt but it does ?pass all the time. This way quickCheck tests terminate,
rather than hang indefinitely, and we can deal with the failed test case
accordingly. TODO: maybe the better way is a more direct output to Z that it
failed so that the ?pass doesn't go to the simulator that tries to do something
with it.  z2p output ensures quickCheck can detect a direct failure and we can
condition properties on it. 

fMulticast no longer crashes on a bad adversary. Before it did a return () when
the same pidR was delivered to in the same instance of fMulticast. Now it does
a ?pass but there is a race condition in the ideal world. Likely in the
simulator. OKAY the cause is taht require does a ?pass but then the main body
also does a ?pass. Therefore, require not returns a Bool and the main body
conditions further operations on the return value.

some things can't be unstructured like the set of all possible strings for an agreement protocol to attempt

sees like also need to ensure all environment actons are "always executable" so that semantically bad input is ignores (like require statements failing) so that theprotocol can catch erors rather than halt and hang forever


** Very cool that fuzz testing helps detects corner cases, esp in error
   handling by forcing race conditions in program print statements. Helps to
   enforce the write-after-read when it isn't checked in the type system.

