Perhaps Classify what different types of structure can give you
================================================================
Here we refer to environments that give inputs round by round or whether they throw random input at the protocol and see what happens. 
For protocols like BenOr, though not a serious agreement protocol, we expect round by round input to give agreement with high probability.
>>> such environments are useful for discovering faults where agreement simply can't be reached. what we mean is protocols where, despite, no malicious activity (but crash fault party), the protocol parties
still can't reach agrement. These environments are structured to produce protocol-specific input sequences and therefore are supposed to yield agreement

Unstructured environments throw random input at the protocols to see what happens. Generally, these help identify bugs of valiating messages that are received, being coerced into agreeing on the wrong value.
With malicious parties giving input  


Limitations for replicating Jepsen
==================================
there is not inherent notion of time in UC
the way in which messages are delivered to processes, say the Tendermint process, I don't think we can adequately test the multi-threaded processes that tendermint uses to process messages. For example if two messages are delivered within miliseconds of each other we can't test race conditions that arise from this. Basically, at the highest lever we can test logical errors and some out-of-order-message problems but not race conditions within a code base. 
When designing with UC natively, there may be subcomponents that do multi-threaded messages delivery and we test that separately and rely on security through composition to only have to test logical erors in tendermint. 
----

safety: we're contending with asynchronous networks so messages get dropped
    - deliver random subsets of messages
    - give crupt input
    pre (o>1)
    assert (set.size o) == 1

agreement:
    - deliver random subsets of messages
    - give crupt input
    assert n==5

liveness: we care that if everything goes well, does the protocol's logic fail?
    - deliver all messages
    - crupt input not important
    assert n==5


Why can't I get equivcation with 2 crupt with t=1?
--------------------------------------------------
When we have 5 honest and 1 crupt, it takes only one well-placed message to make an honest party
decide something when the threshlds are too low. 
When there are 2 corrupt parties (which the protocol can't hold) one of the parties needs to 
achieve the right order of messages for each of the honest parties to get them all to decide. 
This lowers the probabilities of honest parties deciding anything. This makes sense as an answer.


