Injecting Bugs
==============
    Some class of bugs are not possible to cause violations.  Take the ABA
protool that has an error where protocol parties accept the same message twice
from the same party.  In this case, the ovbious first thought is that the
corrupt party just sends n-t AUX messages to a party and cause it to decide.
However, if the protocol uses the sssid of fMulticast to rely on the (est/aux,
sender, parties, round) then the adversary is limited to sending messages an AUX
message through 1 specific instance of fMulicast which doesn't allow sending two
messages to the same receiver.  What is the implication of this?
    1. Mechanics of UC or how a user chooses to program (set the ssids) can
       affect whether a bug is easier to cactch.
    2. UC machinery is unecessarily standin in the way of bug detection.
    3. There is some resilience to UC where the programmer can buid in these
       kinds of protections by reying on an ideal functionality that offers some
       service. 

Perhaps a consequence of this kind of fault is that some parties decide
prematurely and so the other parties are never able to decide because the other
parties stop participating faithfully?  One thing that we can potentially say
is that it's unknown and unclear how protocols interact when their assumptions
about the other are violated. This can exacerbate finding subtle bugs and
points to decomposing protocol into subcomponents rather than designing on
monolithic protocol like in the case of SBCast and ABA.

The outputs of parties only convey what bit the parties decide on. Therefore,
this information on it's own doesn't aways give an idea about bugs internally.
A lot of the time implementation bugs manifest themselves as:
  * parties fail to do anything
  * parties never advance past the first stage of the round (in the case of ABA,
  parties never advance past EST messages)
  * one set of parties decide when they shouldn't and the rest aren't able to
This begs the question, can we use the adversary leak messages to infer
something going wrong?


In trying to break and catch ABA bugs, I came across places where the text of
the ABA paper was vague and came across a bug in my implementation that didn't
capture the intent of the specification.  In the optimsitic case the
implemented  protocol still works as indended. Even with a crash fault the
protocol still terminats and parties all decide the same value.  However, when
trying to play with the thresholds and break the protocol, I discovered paths
where the protocol doesn't conform to the intent of the spec. This became
apparently only after devising a fuzzing strategy of partitioning parties based
on the value they propose and observing strange behavior where they all still
decided on one value. In a sense, such a test case should fail to cause some
parties to terminate but all generated environments that result in some parties
deciding let to partitioned parties deciding values they never receive any
messages for.  This perhaps speaks in favor of the fuzz testing with UC.

This lead me down a path of thinking of what we're calling a bug and what
failures are actually important to catch.  The simplest class of bugs we can
"inject" are that thresholds are incorrectly set. For example, instead of a
party requiring `n-t` messages in order to take an action we set the threshold
`n-t-1` and hope that fuzzing finds the edge case to exploit it.  When a bug
violates safety, it is clear from honest output to Z that the property was
violated.  This kind of bug is a protocol-level bug, i.e. it violates a state
property of the protocol.  Other bugs, more subtle ones, may not necessarily
manifest themselves in a way that makes it easy to conclude that the bug
exists.  Many bugs in a protocol may only cause some honest parties to never
decide any value. For example, some subset of parties decide and their behavior
afterwards makes it impossible for the others to ever decide any value.  In
such a case, the type of environment chosen/generated is important. An
environment that implements only crash faults and delivers all messages in
every round is expected to always force decision and so the existence of a bug
is known DEFINITIVELY.  If the same protocol behavior arises from environments
that withold some messages from some parties until a later round, then it isn't
clear whether such an environment should result in all parties deciding on a
value. The latter kind of bug may be possible to identify by looking at adverasry
leaks and surmising something protocol-specific.  For example, you define what
"locked" behavior looks like for a protocol party. This might be somethin like
a protocol party keeps outputting the same messages and isn't progressing
through the differet stages of the protocol. 

This raises two questions, each of which merit a discussion:
    1. Given that fuzzing isn't a goal in itself but rather an "experimental
apparatus", do we care about classifying types of environments or giving some
relationships between environment types and what they can catch (it may end up
being very protocol specific) or giving a methodology for how to interpret
results from fuzz testing? 
    2. How do we categorize the kinds of bugs we inject (or try to be more
formal about what bugs weintroduce across the different protocols) and do we
care about bugs that aren't protocol-level bugs?
    "protocol-level bugs" := for example a subprotocol that ABA uses has these properties:
        -- s-termination: all calls to it terminate
        -- s-justification: if some process got v back then an honest process
                            called SB(v, true)
        -- s-uniformity: if some process gets v back then all get v back
        -- s-obligation: if t+1 honest call SB(v, true) then eventually all
                            honest get v back




But what do you classify this bug as? Is this a failure of the programmer to read the spec correctly? Is this a failure of the paper spec and whatever analysis it did to arrive at thresholds? Should we care about the distinction between the two cases?

More subtle, and prolly more interesting or probable, bugs likely wouldn't manifest themselves as a safety violation. In fact, it is more is likely that bugs might prevent certain parties from deciding any output at all. When a generated environment encounters output where some parties decide and some don't it the specific environment strategy chosenbecomes relevant. If the environment strategy is to always deliver all messages in each round of the protocol (in arbitrary order) then it is worth investigating as it should always succeed. If the environment partitions parties like above and some don't decide then it's it might be a problem and it might not. 
We discussed last week that we should think of fuzzing as the experimental apparatus to make our point: "UC fits well into software engineering, just look at how it makes fuzzing distributed systems much easier". 
With that in mind, do we care to say something about how to interpret results or how to relate certain kinds of environments to certain kinds of failures? 



Question about bugs.
====================
some bugs like not checking rounds in ABA, are exploiting assumptions made when
wrting the protocol rather than protocol-level problems: without this
assumption we get concurrency issue because we don't expect messages from
previous rounds at the wrong times. 




Our goal is to present show that UC can be used for traditional software engineering and UC provides benefits in its definition to writing good sotware.
Towards this end we want to argue that successful / tradtional testing techniques like fuzzing can be applied to implementations in UC. 
There is anoter part to this, that we have to motivate that something about UC-ness helps software development. Already discussed trying to emulate paper
definitions as a beneficial goal (which is obvious). Already pointed out that ideal functionalities function as a protcol spec to be used as the abstraction layer between code packages, for example, and havng a spec to test again is built-in with UC.
Simulator proofs can also be validated (TBD) making literature more usable and falsifiable (borrow reasoning from literature about PL tools for UC).

** also good to discover new lemma / properties about the protocol that can be rapidly tested ==> validates proofs / properties  

